hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2955","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to me 'Amr ibn 'Asim, narrated to me Abu al-'Awwam — and his name is 'Imran ibn Dawud al-Qattan — narrated to me Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik (RA), who said:

I witnessed the Prophet (ﷺ) during the Farewell Pilgrimage while he was delivering a sermon. A man came to him and said, ""I forgot to throw the pebbles (during the stoning of the Jamarat)."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""Throw them, and there is no harm.""

Then another man came and said, ""I forgot to perform the Tawaf (circumambulation of the Ka'bah)."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""Perform the Tawaf, and there is no harm.""

Then another man came and said, ""I forgot to slaughter the sacrificial animal."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""Slaughter it, and there is no harm.""

The Prophet (ﷺ) was not asked about anything that day except that he would say, ""There is no harm.""

He (ﷺ) also said, ""Indeed, Allah has removed the hardship (al-haraj), except in the case of a person who deliberately takes something that belongs to a Muslim, for that is a sin.""

**CHAPTER:**"
